{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Hark hark good traveler let mine quill trace thy voyage through the fair and ancient isles of Japan where cherry blossoms do dance with the zephyrs and temples whisper tales of old.\n\nOn the morn of thine first day when the cock doth crow and the sun doth peek o’er yonder mountains dost thou arrive in the bustling realm of Tokyo fair. Here in the heart of neon and tradition thou shalt tarry. At dawn do visit the sacred Meiji Shrine where kamidana gods do dwell and the towering torii gates call thee onward. Walk through the emerald forest where none but gods and pilgrims tread. Then to the teeming streets of Shinjuku where lights do wink like stars fallen to earth. Let thy belly feast upon ramen rich as a samurai’s dreams or on sushi so fresh it doth glisten like the waves of Edo Bay by old.\n\nUpon the morrow dost thou embark for the ancient city of Kyoto where the past doth linger like a lover’s sigh. At break of day dost thou rise and make haste to the Golden Pavilion where gold doth gleam like the sun upon the waters of Kyoto’s mirror pond. Then to the silver temple of Kinkaku-ji where reflections dance upon the lake’s surface. Visit next the Arashiyama bamboo grove where stalks do sway and whisper secrets to the wind. In the eventide dost thou dine on kaiseki a feast fit for emperors of yore with dishes that sing of season and soil.\n\nOn the third day let thy heart be guided to Nara where deer do wander like gentle knights of old and the Great Buddha doth smile upon all who seek solace. Feed the deer with crackers bought from vendors with voices sweet as temple bells. Then to the Todai-ji temple where the bronze colossus of Buddha doth stand guard o’er the land. Let thy spirit be stirred by the echoes of a thousand prayers.\n\nThe fourth day shall see thee journey to Hiroshima where the peace of time is guarded by the children’s monument. Stand beneath the dome where shadows of sorrow do rest and let thy heart bear witness to the flowers that bloom anew. Then travel to Miyajima where the floating torii of Itsukushima doth rise from the tide like a bridge ‘twixt earth and heaven. At dusk dost thou see the lanterns glow upon the water and knowst thou hast beheld a sight most wondrous.\n\nOn the fifth day return thou to the modern marvels of Osaka where castles stand proudly and street food doth tempt thee at every turn. Visit Osaka Castle where the spirits of warriors do patrol the ramparts. Then to Dotonbori where neon signs do shout and takoyaki and okonomiyaki do beckon like sirens of old.\n\nThe sixth day shall find thee in the land of hot springs at Hakone where the earth doth breathe forth steam and relaxation. Bathe in the onsen where thy cares shall melt like snow upon a hearth. Then gaze upon Mount Fuji if the mists do allow her crown to be seen.\n\nAnd so good traveler let thy journey end in Tokyo once more where the last night shall be spent amidst the hum of a thousand dreams. Let thy heart be filled with the stories of temples and treachery samurai and serenity where every street and shrine hath taught thee a lesson. Depart with the words of the great playwright ever in thy mind “All the world’s a stage and all the men and women merely players” for thou hast walked the stage of Japan and played thy part with grace.\n\nSafe travels and fare thee well.", "logprobs": [{"token": "H", "logprob": -0.1340528428554535}, {"token": "ark", "logprob": -0.004188099876046181}, {"token": " h", "logprob": -1.7543089389801025}, {"token": "ark", "logprob": -0.0038423535879701376}, {"token": " good", "logprob": -0.3934949040412903}, {"token": " travel", "logprob": -0.5135269165039062}, {"token": "er", "logprob": -0.0007915939204394817}, {"token": " let", "logprob": -2.1779160499572754}, {"token": " mine", "logprob": -0.9581542015075684}, {"token": " qu", "logprob": -0.38110336661338806}, {"token": "ill"